data: {"seq":78,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:46:02.680Z","text":"The trace came back empty diff—the request port 80 but the trace used port 10080? The trigger_result shows \"kind: tcp_connect, port: 10080\". That's odd—the trace harness remapped. Anyway, empty diff means the request didn't reach the handler. The trace request had path \"/cgi?1\" — maybe httpd needs a proper host header or the harness maps port. Actually the payload_bytes:0 — nothing was sent. The trace sent a TCP connect but no payload (port 80 mapped to 10080). Empty diff.\n\nFor the httpd cmdexec, my filter returned only 1 path with binary 08b086... (not httpd). So the httpd binary doesn't have high-scored cmdexec paths, or they didn't make top 50. This is a big firmware. TP-Link httpd command injection is typically via cgi param turning into system() with NVRAM values.\n\nLet me reconsider. The top attack surface paths were in binaries 01312699 (maybe an ffmpeg/rtsp?), 08b08601, 0053d7fc. Those weren't httpd. Those look like other binaries (maybe a media/rtsp daemon). Let me reconsider the highest-value targets.\n\nActually, the trace harness maps port 80 → 10080 automatically. The empty diff means httpd didn't process /cgi?1. That may be because httpd requires the request to hit a valid route and possibly a Host header, or the connect succeeded but no proper HTTP request was sent (payload_bytes 0). Let me send an actual request with payload. The fw_request_trace via=net with request_path sends an HTTP GET. But payload_bytes:0 suggests it sent connect only? Strange.\n\nLet me re-approach. Maybe focus on identifying the actual highest-confidence network binaries. The most interesting targets could be:\n- dnsproxy (UDP DNS) — could have overflow\n- minidlnad (UPnP/SSDP)\n- ipcserver\n- uclited\n\nActually, command injection in these TP-Link binaries is the classic target. But the big prize: the httpd. Let me look at how trace works — maybe I need to give a full HTTP request via payload on port. Let me retry the trace with a proper HTTP request payload via via=net but with payload (raw bytes). Actually request_path via net auto-sends GET. The empty diff with 0 payload bytes suggests the harness's port mapping connected but the GET wasn't sent. \n\nLet me try fw_qemu_exec on httpd to check reachability, and try a trace with an actual payload string for HTTP. Actually let me use payload with payload string for an HTTP request. Let me check what the harness supports: request_path sends payload on the port (HTTP). Let me retry with a valid-looking path like \"/\" and check.\n\nHmm, but empty diff on first attempt. Let me consider the possibility that httpd requires argv/argv0. Actually let me just try fw_request_trace via=net with port 80 and request_path \"/cgi?2\" again but with a payload post. Actually let me try giving a payload via the GET.\n\nLet me reconsider — the httpd in this firmware may fork per connection, and the harness may need the request. The trigger_result said tcp_connect port 10080. The harness remapped :80 to :10080. It sent 0 payload bytes though. Possibly the request_path mechanism failed to produce payload. Let me instead use payload parameter with a full HTTP request.\n\nActually let me step back and think about which input gives the best ROI. This is Archer C7. Known TP-Link vulns: many command injections in the cgi \"formSubmit\" or in the login. But let me be systematic.\n\nLet me check which binaries actually have network cmdexec and are the httpd-like web. Let me look at the binaries list and search within httpd for system/popen callers (handlers). Let me search for functions containing \"system\" in httpd.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: use fw_get_function_source on the httpd's dangerous functions. Let me search httpd for \"popen\" and \"system\" call sites.\n\nLet me retry the trace with a proper payload first (since dynamic is hard step). Let me send an HTTP GET with Host header. I'll use payload string via net with port 80.","stream":false,"block":0}

