event: thinking
data: {"seq":130,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:46:32.715Z","text":"The httpd is complex: it creates control lists for /userRpm/, /fs/, /incoming/, etc. The final handler is httpRpmFs for /fs/ (file serving) and generic list dispatch for RPMs (userRpm). \n\nThe aliases: / → /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm. /userRpm/* requires admin. httpFileSetDefaultFs(1).\n\nFor auth bypass and command injection, the classic TP-Link pattern is in the /userRpm/* RPM handlers. But those need admin creds. However, TP-Link has had auth bypass where certain RPMs don't require auth.\n\nGiven the complexity and qemu issues, let me take a step back. Maybe a better dynamic target is one of the simpler daemons. But the httpd is the most valuable (pre-auth RCE, auth bypass).\n\nThe qemu empty diff with recv 0 suggests httpd didn't even reach httpd()/accept — maybe because httpInit needs config files, or the harness ran httpd with no args and it chdi","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":131,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:46:33.103Z","text":"The httpd is complex: it creates control lists for /userRpm/, /fs/, /incoming/, etc. The final handler is httpRpmFs for /fs/ (file serving) and generic list dispatch for RPMs (userRpm). \n\nThe aliases: / → /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm. /userRpm/* requires admin. httpFileSetDefaultFs(1).\n\nFor auth bypass and command injection, the classic TP-Link pattern is in the /userRpm/* RPM handlers. But those need admin creds. However, TP-Link has had auth bypass where certain RPMs don't require auth.\n\nGiven the complexity and qemu issues, let me take a step back. Maybe a better dynamic target is one of the simpler daemons. But the httpd is the most valuable (pre-auth RCE, auth bypass).\n\nThe qemu empty diff with recv 0 suggests httpd didn't even reach httpd()/accept — maybe because httpInit needs config files, or the harness ran httpd with no args and it chdir /tmp then checks needKeepAlive which calls system etc. Actually httpd() registers conf then httpServerStart. Under qemu-user, getModelParameters may fail (needs /proc or nvram) so main returns -1 without starting server. That's why recv","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":132,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:46:33.463Z","text":"The httpd is complex: it creates control lists for /userRpm/, /fs/, /incoming/, etc. The final handler is httpRpmFs for /fs/ (file serving) and generic list dispatch for RPMs (userRpm). \n\nThe aliases: / → /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm. /userRpm/* requires admin. httpFileSetDefaultFs(1).\n\nFor auth bypass and command injection, the classic TP-Link pattern is in the /userRpm/* RPM handlers. But those need admin creds. However, TP-Link has had auth bypass where certain RPMs don't require auth.\n\nGiven the complexity and qemu issues, let me take a step back. Maybe a better dynamic target is one of the simpler daemons. But the httpd is the most valuable (pre-auth RCE, auth bypass).\n\nThe qemu empty diff with recv 0 suggests httpd didn't even reach httpd()/accept — maybe because httpInit needs config files, or the harness ran httpd with no args and it chdir /tmp then checks needKeepAlive which calls system etc. Actually httpd() registers conf then httpServerStart. Under qemu-user, getModelParameters may fail (needs /proc or nvram) so main returns -1 without starting server. That's why recv 0.\n\nSo httpd might not be runnable in this qemu environment without the platform tools, OR I need to pass the right input. This is an environment limitation for the full tester.\n\nGiven that, perhaps I should focus on targets that DO run under qemu. Let me try a trace on a simpler daemon. Actually, let me reconsider","stream":true,"block":0}

