data: {"seq":136,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:46:35.161Z","text":"The httpd is complex: it creates control lists for /userRpm/, /fs/, /incoming/, etc. The final handler is httpRpmFs for /fs/ (file serving) and generic list dispatch for RPMs (userRpm). \n\nThe aliases: / → /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm. /userRpm/* requires admin. httpFileSetDefaultFs(1).\n\nFor auth bypass and command injection, the classic TP-Link pattern is in the /userRpm/* RPM handlers. But those need admin creds. However, TP-Link has had auth bypass where certain RPMs don't require auth.\n\nGiven the complexity and qemu issues, let me take a step back. Maybe a better dynamic target is one of the simpler daemons. But the httpd is the most valuable (pre-auth RCE, auth bypass).\n\nThe qemu empty diff with recv 0 suggests httpd didn't even reach httpd()/accept — maybe because httpInit needs config files, or the harness ran httpd with no args and it chdir /tmp then checks needKeepAlive which calls system etc. Actually httpd() registers conf then httpServerStart. Under qemu-user, getModelParameters may fail (needs /proc or nvram) so main returns -1 without starting server. That's why recv 0.\n\nSo httpd might not be runnable in this qemu environment without the platform tools, OR I need to pass the right input. This is an environment limitation for the full tester.\n\nGiven that, perhaps I should focus on targets that DO run under qemu. Let me try a trace on a simpler daemon. Actually, let me reconsider the identification inputs: dhcp6c, dnsproxy, ipcserver, minidlnad, uclited.\n\nLet me look at which one is easiest and high-value. Command injection candidates: TP-Link's various daemons. Actually let me check binary 08b08601 — start_pppd cmdexec. Which binary is it? Let me identify by checking list. It wasn't in the visible portion of the binaries list. Let me search the list for it. Actually I can't filter the list. Let me just check the top attack surface paths — the memunsafe ones in 01312699 (libavformat) — those are UDP/RTSP network parsers reachable. \n\nActually, given the difficulty, let me focus on the httpd since it's the main web. Even if full boot fails, I might be able to drive it via fw_qemu_exec with specific argv to at least confirm something, or the harness's httpd might need argv. Actually the trace ran httpd with argv=[] (no args). main with argc=1... wait argc would be 1 (just program name). Actually argv empty means qemu runs `/usr/bin/httpd` with no extra args, so argc=1. In main: argc>=3 no. getopt loop with argc=1 returns -1 immediately. argc","stream":true,"block":0}

