data: {"seq":161,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:46:49.031Z","text":"The high-scored paths are in 01312699 (libavformat, ffmpeg) and 08b08601 (a PPP/voice-related binary with network_thread, do_control, get_call — likely a \"dial\"/\"call\" daemon, maybe \"3g\" or \"dyna\" dialer) and 0053d7fc.\n\nBinary 08b08601: network_thread, do_control, get_call, start_pppd, dial_no_tmp. This looks like a 3G dialer or a \"phantom\" - could be \"sbin/3gdial\" or similar. Where's it in the inputs? It wasn't in the identification list shown (only 15 inputs listed). Hmm, the identification had 15 inputs, and 08b08601... let me check: not in the listed inputs. But it's a network binary. Weird. Maybe it's related to a serial/network device.\n\nActually, this binary with network_thread and do_control - it might be \"tl3gdial\" or a PPPoE dialer. Hard to drive without knowing protocol.\n\nGiven the analysis is static-only with unclear dataflow, I should focus on getting a real dynamic-verifiable finding. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe best ROI target for the user's goals (auth bypass, cmd injection, pre-auth RCE) on a TP-Link router is definitely the httpd. But it doesn't run in qemu. Let me at least confirm whether httpd can be executed at all with fw_qemu_exec to see if main starts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the harness. The trace for httpd returned \"tcp_connect port 10080\" and sent payload, recv 0. This means qemu spawned httpd, it bound (or tried) to port 10080, the harness connected, sent bytes, but httpd didn't respond. If httpd's main exits before httpServerStart (getModelParameters failed), there'd be no listening port and connect would fail. But connect succeeded (tcp_connect). So httpd DID start listening (httpServerStart ran) but didn't process/respond to the request. \n\nWait, but recv 0 and empty diff. If httpd is listening and received the request, the request-handling code should show in coverage diff. Unless the request parsing happens in a child process/thread that qemu-user single-threaded tracing doesn't capture well, OR the request needs specific format.\n\nThe httpd uses httpDispatcher which matches URL patterns against rpm lists. My request \"/cgi?2\" wouldn't match any alias (they're /userRpm/, /fs/, /login/, /incoming/, /). Actually \"/\" aliases to /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm. A GET / would hit LoginRpm. My \"/cgi?2\" matches no alias → 501 or redirect. It would still process. But empty diff.\n\nThe issue: httpServerStart probably forks/threads per connection and the qemu trace may not follow the child. Or the httpd request handler uses a worker thread that needs NVRAM values.\n\nLet me try a trace on the login route \"/\" which is the primary path. Let me send GET / HTTP/1.1. That maps to LoginRpm.htm which is in the RPM list and doesn't require auth (login page). This could at least show coverage.\n\nBut wait — the earlier traces with /cgi?2 got recv 0. Maybe httpd needs the Host header or a proper full request. I gave a full GET request with Host. Still 0.\n\nLet me check if maybe the httpd needs argv like \"-f\" or runs correctly. Actually, let me try fw_qemu_exec on httpd to see if it executes (does it even get to listen). Let me do a quick exec.\n\nActually, given budget constraints (traces max 192, qemu 24, fuzz 12), let me be more strategic. \n\nLet me step back: the input inventory lists many daemons. The one that's a known TP-Link pre-auth RCE target and easy to drive could be the ftp (vsftpd) or ssh (dropbear) — but those are standard. \n\nGiven the difficulty, let me focus on the httpd login flow which is the highest-value. Let me try driving a valid login request to see if httpd processes it. Let me try trace with GET / instead.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether httpd actually reached processing. Let me look at httpServerStart and the accept loop. The earlier AS-014 mentioned sub_4459C0 as an accept func. And wmnet* functions. Let me just try a trace with \"/\" path to hit the login RPM.\n\nActually, I've spent 2 traces on httpd already, both empty. Per rules, don't repeat same port+payload. Let me try a different approach: use fw_qemu_exec to confirm httpd runs, and use a trace with the login path.\n\nLet me try fw_qemu_exec on httpd first to see if it starts and whether it's the environment.\n\nActually let me reconsider time budget. Let me loo","stream":true,"block":0}

