data: {"seq":232,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:47:36.941Z","text":"dns_decode_request copies DNS packet data into fixed buffers using strncpy with fixed lengths (0x2E, 0xFF) — these are bounded, so no obvious overflow. This looks like the \"dns_decode_request\" only handles the a1+... structure where a1 is a large struct (offset 3204, 524*i array). This is the DNS response decode.\n\ndnsproxy is likely a \"smart DNS\" or DDNS resolver. The cmdexec dispatcher sub_406450 = sys_run_command — where does that come from? conf_cmdparse uses strtok/sscanf. This is config parsing (from file or argv probably). start_pppd cmdexec was in the other binary.\n\ndnsproxy path seems config-driven. Hard to get a strong finding.\n\nLet me reconsider. I'm burning budget. Let me think about the log: I have spent ~4 traces. \n\nActually — a more promising realization: the search showed routes /userRpm/CmxDdnsRpm.htm, /userRpm/DynDdnsRpm.htm, /userRpm/NoipDdnsRpm.htm, /userRpm/PeanutHullDdnsRpm.htm. These are DDNS RPM handlers in... which binary? They're routes registered globally (probably in httpd). DDNS handlers are classic command injection in TP-Link (domain name, username, password passed to nvram_set or curl/system). But httpd doesn't run in qemu.\n\nLet me reconsider the priority of finding something dynamic. Given httpd is not runnable, and other daemons like dnsproxy are config-driven, let me look at minidlnad which parses SSDP/SOAP XML and runs standalone.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider whether I can still record static findings. The instructions allow static-only findings capped at 0.7 confidence. The top attack-surface paths are static_only. I could record a well-analyzed static finding if I can trace a clear dataflow to a sink. \n\nBut per discipline, I should aim for dynamic verification. Let me pursue the highest-value tractable dynamic target.\n\nLet me reconsider: The dhcp6c/dhcp6s (DHCPv6) are standard-ish. bpalogin is a client. ipcserver — a TCP IPC daemon. uclited.\n\nGiven constraints, let me focus on getting one solid dynamic finding. Let me check minidlnad since UPnP SSDP is pre-auth and parseable. Let me look at minidlnad's surface (AS-008) and its SOAP/parser.\n\nActually, let me reconsider budget. I've used 4 trace attempts (all httpd, all empty). Let me check the trace quota isn't near exhaustion. The instructions say quota is what the tool returns. Let me not worry yet.\n\nLet me pursue minidlnad. Its surface said AS-008. Let me get it and its main parser. Actually the surface gate said AS-008 warning? Let me recheck: warnings listed AS-009, AS-006, AS-003 no dispatcher. AS-008 not warned. Let me get AS-008 and check minidlnad's dispatch.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what's realistic in this session. The strongest candidate for a REAL confirmed vuln with dynamic evidence is one where the binary runs under qemu and has a reachable overflow. \n\nLet me instead try a different tack: the minidlnad is a full DLNA server (media). It has XML parsing (UPnP SOAP). Known TP-Link/CVE? Let me look.\n\nAlternatively, I could pursue libavformat (01312699) network parsers via a harness — but it's a library.\n\nLet me look at minidlnad surface and identify a concrete parse function.\n\nLet me get AS-008 and search minidlnad for UPnP/SOAP handling.","stream":false,"block":0}

