data: {"seq":240,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:47:42.046Z","text":"sub_403AF8 is xl2tpd code (l2tp_log, control_finish, gethostbyname, tunnel). So 08b08601 is xl2tpd. It has network_thread, do_control, get_call, start_pppd — it's xl2tpd (L2TP daemon, LAC). network_thread is the L2TP control packet handler. This parses L2TP control messages (AVPs) from UDP port 1701. That's a pre-auth network parser on the L2TP tunnel.\n\nBut wait, identification exclu","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":241,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:47:42.643Z","text":"sub_403AF8 is xl2tpd code (l2tp_log, control_finish, gethostbyname, tunnel). So 08b08601 is xl2tpd. It has network_thread, do_control, get_call, start_pppd — it's xl2tpd (L2TP daemon, LAC). network_thread is the L2TP control packet handler. This parses L2TP control messages (AVPs) from UDP port 1701. That's a pre-auth network parser on the L2TP tunnel.\n\nBut wait, identification excluded xl2tpd as \"infrastructure/control daemon — not public\". But it does listen on UDP 1701 for L2TP. Actually L2TP control is on UDP 1701 both directions. As a LAC (dialer), it connects out. Reachability is limited.\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me focus. The minidlnad (UPnP) is the strongest pre-auth target. It has:\n- Process_upnphttp — HTTP accept\n- ProcessSSDPRequest — SSDP\n- parsexml, ParseNameValue\n- several sys_run_command (cmdexec sinks!) — sub_405594=sys_run_command, sub_405D5C=sys_run_command_2, sub_416168=sys_run_c","stream":true,"block":0}

