data: {"seq":265,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:48:03.005Z","text":"minidlnad fails to start: \"fatal: No IP address automatically detected!\" — it needs an interface IP. In qemu-user there's no network interface. This daemon requires a real interface. So it won't run under qemu either.\n\nGiven many daemons need real interfaces/config, the dynamically-drivable set is limited. Let me think about what actually runs.\n\nMaybe I should revisit the httpd — it at least bound a port (tcp_connect succeeded on 10080) implying httpServerStart ran and it's listening, but the request handling produced recv 0. Wait, but qemu_exec of httpd failed with \"Firmware version check failed\". Contradiction: in the trace, connect succeeded (so listening), but in qemu_exec it exited. Perhaps the trace harness provides some stubs or the httpd in trace mode reached listen but the request handler needs the worker threads that fail (pthread_create failed).\n\nHmm. The \"pthread_create failed !\" in httpd exec is concerning — httpd spawns worker threads; under qemu maybe threads fail. But connect succeeded in trace. Confusing, but recv 0 means not processing.\n\nLet me reconsider. Given dynamic environment constraints on most firmware daemons, maybe my best avenue is:\n1. Static analysis on a clear command-injection path in the DDNS RPM handlers of httpd (very common TP-Link vuln), record as static-only.\n2. Or find a daemon that DOES run.\n\nLet me reconsider which inputs run. The identification services that are pure clients or simple might run. dhcp6s is a server. Let me think about which one doesn't need network interface/ip detection.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe I'm over-focused on the .bin's fake environment. The daemons that need interface detection (minidlnad, httpd) won't run. \n\nLet me look at ipcserver (TCP IPC) — a simple TCP server that processes commands. It might run standalone. Let me check its surface and whether it runs.\n\nActually, a cleaner idea: Let me look at the DDNS/command-injection family in httpd statically and confirm with strong static evidence, since httpd is the main attack surface (pre-auth? DDNS requires login though).\n\nAlternatively - let me reconsider the xl2tpd network_thread. xl2tpd listens on UDP 1701 for L2TP. As a PTA (LNS) it would accept tunnels from WAN. As LAC it connects out. network_thread parses received L2TP control messages. This is a genuine pre-auth UDP parser. It might run under qemu (xl2tpd might not need interface IP to start, though it binds specific IPs).\n\nLet me reconsider my budget and be decisive. I'll pursue the strongest clear-cut finding:\n\nOption A: httpd DDNS command injection (static). \nOption B: xl2tpd L2TP control parsing (dynamic if runs).\n\nGiven the difficulty, let me actually check whether any of these run via fw_qemu_exec for a couple candidates quickly,","stream":true,"block":0}

