data: {"seq":305,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:48:28.417Z","text":"These init functions are just constructor callbacks, not real processing. ipcserver is small. I'm spreading too thin. Let me refocus decisively.\n\nGiven the environment can't run httpd, minidlnad, xl2tpd (they need interfaces/config). The achievable dynamic targets are limited. Let me reconsider what actually runs.\n\nActually, I recall from the httpd trace: tcp_connect on port 10080 SUCCEEDED. That means httpd DID bind and listen in the trace environment (which may mock more than fw_qemu_exec). But recv 0 — httpd didn't send a response. That could be because the request went to a worker thread that qemu-user's coverage (single-threaded) couldn't trace, OR httpd genuinely didn't process.\n\nWait — actually maybe httpd did process but in a forked child, and the diff harness tracks only the main process. The recv 0 though suggests no response generated.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Perhaps I need to send the request in a way httpd expects. TP-Link httpd sometimes requires the request to be a valid URL starting with /userRpm/ etc. My \"/\" should work (login page).\n\nActually, the empty diff + recv 0 for BOTH httpd and (not tested) suggests the trace harness runs the binary but the daemon needs the mocked env. But connect succeeded on 10080. This is contradictory unless httpd bound but then the request handling is in a thread.\n\nLet me try a truly different approach: use a fuzz run on a network parser. Actually, fw_request_fuzz whole-binary mode on a parser binary.\n\nI'm going in circles. Let me make a decision: pursue the xl2tpd L2TP control UDP parsing as a dynamic target since xl2tpd at least is a real UDP-1701 parser, OR reconsider httpd with a fuzz.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I should just try the trace on a binary that genuinely runs. Among the candidates, `dhcp6s` (DHCPv6 server) is a standard daemon that parses DHCPv6 client requests over UDP 547/546. dhcp6d often runs with fixed config. Let me check if it runs.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. Given budget spent, let me pivot to something I can actually verify. Let me reconsider the httpd more carefully — its main does `getModelParameters()`. Under the trace harness, maybe the model file is present. The connect DID succeed (tcp_connect port 10080). So httpd is listening in trace mode. The recv 0 might be because the worker that handles the connection needs time or the request parsing never matched.\n\nLet me try the httpd trace once","stream":true,"block":0}

