 me check if it runs.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. Given budget spent, let me pivot to something I can actually verify. Let me reconsider the httpd more carefully — its main does `getModelParameters()`. Under the trace harness, maybe the model file is present. The connect DID succeed (tcp_connect port 10080). So httpd is listening in trace mode. The recv 0 might be because the worker that handles the connection needs time or the request parsing never matched.\n\nLet me try the httpd trace once more with a valid route that definitely processes: GET /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm. This maps to the login RPM which is in the list and doesn't require auth. This should at least invoke httpDispatcher for LoginRpm.htm. Different payload from before.\n\nBut I've done 3 httpd traces (2 empty diff, one /cgi, one /). The rule says don't repeat same port+payload; /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm is a new payload. Let me try it. Meanwhile, let me also examine a cmdexec sink in httpd to know what to look for.\n\nActually, I realize the httpd trace output for the FIRST trace (5fb6f3470768) and second (4cf87467f890) both sent payload and got recv 0. So httpd is listening but silent. This strongly suggests the request handler is in the worker thread model and qemu-user single process trace isn't capturing it — OR httpd's HTTP parse expects a specific thing.\n\nLet me try trace with /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm.\n\nAlso, to make progress on findings, let me look at a clear command injection in httpd statically. Let me look at the DDNS handler. Search for CmxDdns or the Rpms that build commands.\n\nGiven one-trace-at-a-time, let me fire the httpd trace with LoginRpm.htm path, and search httpd for nvram_get + system patterns.\n\nActually, let me be efficient: fire the httpd LoginRpm trace and simultaneously read a promising httpd cmdexec function. Let me search httpd for functions named like \"*Rpm*\" handlers. Let me instead directly examine a known dangerous pattern: many TP-Link handlers call `nvram_get` then `sprintf(cmd, \"killall ... %s\", val)` then `system(cmd)`. Let me search httpd for \"Rpm\" function names.\n\nLet me do the trace + search \"Rpm\".","stream":false,"block":0}

