data: {"seq":405,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:49:51.774Z","text":"dnsproxy also empty (it uses UDP, but the trace via=net showed \"tcp_payload\" kind, meaning it's connecting via TCP or the harness handles it as payload but dnsproxy may not have bound because it needs config, or it does select on dns_sock which might not be set up). recv 0. dnsproxy likely needs the 'config' from resolv.conf and init may have failed.\n\nSo dnsproxy also doesn't respond. These daemons need the init to set up sockets which requires reading /tmp/resolv.conf etc.\n\nI've now spent a lot of budget with no dynamic success. The environment can't run the firmware daemons meaningfully (they need nvram/config/interfaces).\n\nLet me reconsider. Given the environment constraints, the realistic deliverable is a well-verified static finding. The user explicitly listed command injection, memory corruption, auth bypass, arbitrary file read/write, info disclosure, pre-auth RCE as goals. I should pick the best static candidate and record it with strong evidence.\n\nBest candidates from the attack surface:\n1. ff_rtsp_connect in libavformat (0x6d968): strcpy(dest, &s[1601]) with s[1601] being a 1600+ byte buffer copied from network into dest — this is a potential buffer overflow in RTSP URL handling. But it's in a library used by minidlnad.\n2. The httpd file serving path traversal.\n3. The httpd DDNS command injection.\n\nHowever, static-only confidence is capped 0.7. To have a credible finding I need a clear source→sink with actual dataflow, not speculative.\n\nLet me look at a concrete and real one. Let me examine the minidlnad processes which have sys_run_command sinks. Even if minidlnad doesn't run in qemu, a clear command injection with network source would be a strong static finding and could be fuzzed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the minidlnad SendResp_dlnafile / file serving (path traversal) which is a classic. And check for command injection in its SOAP handlers.\n\nBut first, let me reconsider the whole picture — maybe I should check the fuzz capability on a parser to get dynamic evidence even if the daemon's full boot fails.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the minidlnad. It failed because \"No IP address automatically detected\". But maybe with argv I can give it an interface/config","stream":true,"block":0}

