data: {"seq":415,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:49:59.399Z","text":"dnsproxy also empty (it uses UDP, but the trace via=net showed \"tcp_payload\" kind, meaning it's connecting via TCP or the harness handles it as payload but dnsproxy may not have bound because it needs config, or it does select on dns_sock which might not be set up). recv 0. dnsproxy likely needs the 'config' from resolv.conf and init may have failed.\n\nSo dnsproxy also doesn't respond. These daemons need the init to set up sockets which requires reading /tmp/resolv.conf etc.\n\nI've now spent a lot of budget with no dynamic success. The environment can't run the firmware daemons meaningfully (they need nvram/config/interfaces).\n\nLet me reconsider. Given the environment constraints, the realistic deliverable is a well-verified static finding. The user explicitly listed command injection, memory corruption, auth bypass, arbitrary file read/write, info disclosure, pre-auth RCE as goals. I should pick the best static candidate and record it with strong evidence.\n\nBest candidates from the attack surface:\n1. ff_rtsp_connect in libavformat (0x6d968): strcpy(dest, &s[1601]) with s[1601] being a 1600+ byte buffer copied from network into dest — this is a potential buffer overflow in RTSP URL handling. But it's in a library used by minidlnad.\n2. The httpd file serving path traversal.\n3. The httpd DDNS command injection.\n\nHowever, static-only confidence is capped 0.7. To have a credible finding I need a clear source→sink with actual dataflow, not speculative.\n\nLet me look at a concrete and real one. Let me examine the minidlnad processes which have sys_run_command sinks. Even if minidlnad doesn't run in qemu, a clear command injection with network source would be a strong static finding and could be fuzzed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the minidlnad SendResp_dlnafile / file serving (path traversal) which is a classic. And check for command injection in its SOAP handlers.\n\nBut first, let me reconsider the whole picture — maybe I should check the fuzz capability on a parser to get dynamic evidence even if the daemon's full boot fails.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the minidlnad. It failed because \"No IP address automatically detected\". But maybe with argv I can give it an interface/config. minidlnad uses -i <interface> or a config file. But it still needs a real ifindex which qemu doesn't provide.\n\nGiven the constraints, I think the best move is to focus on a clean static finding and verify what I can. Let me look at the minidlnad SOAP control handler sub_4147B0 (http_handle_schemas_xmlsoap_org) and the sys_run_command sinks to find a command injection reachable from a SOAP/SSDP request (pre-auth UPnP).\n\nBut minidlnad is the media server — SOAP control actions are like Browse. Command injection there is less common.\n\nAlternatively, let me look at the minidlnad parsexml / sub_40D1FC which parses the HTTP request. Path traversal in file serving (SendResp_dlnafile) is a real known minidlna class of bug.\n\nHonestly, let me pivot to something I can actually verify dynamically: use fw_request_fuzz on a specific function in a library or binary. The fuzz tool can run whole-binary mode on MIPS. Let me fuzz dnsproxy or an httpd parser function.\n\nActually, given the difficulty and budget, let me reconsider using fw_request_fuzz on a concrete parser function. But fuzz on these needs the function to be callable in isolation.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's achievable and valuable. The user's target is presumably one real vulnerability. Let me pursue the highest-confidence static path and record it, while being honest about reachability=static-only.\n\nLet me examine the ff_rtsp_connect in libavformat (01312699) — this is in libavformat and the ai_review said strcpy(dest, &s[1601]). Let me read it fully to see if it's a real overflow. Actually this is used by minidlnad when it connects to RTSP streams or by other media binaries. Network source is the URL pointing to attacker-controlled server. It's a stretch.\n\nLet me instead look at the httpd's arbitrary file read via path traversal. The httpd maps /fs/* to /rc_filesys/doc/* and httpRpmFs serves files. The final_handler httpFileRegHandle. If there's path traversal (../), arbitrary file read. But /fs/* requires \"admin\" auth. Still, it's the main web.\n\nHmm, but authentication bypass is one of the user's goals.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the cleanest findable issue is in the httpd but I can't verify it. \n\nLet me reconsider budget and decision. I've used ~7 traces, 3 qemu execs. Trace budget max 192, so plenty. qemu 24. Unix. Let me not give up on dynamic yet.\n\nLet me reconsider which binary will actually run. The daemons that failed all need network interface/config. But maybe a simpler one that parses stdin or a file will run. \n\nActually, the smartest move: re-examine the httpd trace issue. In the FIRST trace, trigger_result was \"tcp_connect port 10080\" — connect succeeded. That means httpd bound. But recv 0. \n\nWhat if httpd requires the connection to be kept open and the response comes after the request is fully read including closing? Or httpd's socket group wait (wmnetSocketGroupWait) uses select with a long timeout. The harness sends payload then waits ~8s. httpd might respond but the harness reads 0 because httpd wrote before harness recv or httpd needs more time.\n\nActually the harness \"recv: 0\" means it read 0 bytes after sending. That suggests httpd genuinely produced no output. \n\nGiven httpd threads, maybe the worker thread handling the connection crashed. In qemu_user, httpd spawns worker threads with pthread; the exec showed \"pthread_create failed !\". So httpd can't create its worker threads → it can't process requests → recv 0. That's the root cause. So httpd is dynamically dead.\n\nOK. Let me pivot to finding a static finding that's solid, OR find a daemon that runs single-threaded.\n\nGiven the many dead ends, let me pursue the strongest static candidate and record it. Let me examine the minidlnad HTTP request parsing for a clear overflow or path traversal, which is well-understood.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the minidlnad's HTTP parser sub_40D1FC and the upnphttp request reading. Let me look at Process_upnphttp to see how it reads the request and whether there's an unbounded copy. Let me read Process_upnphttp and sub_40D1FC.\n\nActually, I realize I should focus my remaining effort on producing at least one solid finding. Let me look at minidlnad's request handling for a buffer overflow in reading the HTTP request line (a classic minidlna vuln — CVE-2020-XXXX in Process_upnphttp / GetHeaderLine). Let me read the upnphttp read function.\n\nLet me read sub_40D1FC and find the function that reads the request into a fixed buffer.\n\nLet me get minidlnad's Process_upnphttp source.","stream":true,"block":0}

