that needs full init is hard too.\n\nLet me reconsider budget and pick ONE target to drive to a recordable finding. Given the user's explicit interests (auth bypass, cmd injection, arbitrary file RW, info disclosure, pre-auth RCE), the best match remains httpd. Even though I can't run it, its static analysis for command injection is very tractable and well-known.\n\nLet me find a concrete command injection in httpd. Let me look at a specific handler. The httpd uses \"nvram_get\" + \"system\"/execFormatCmd. Let me search httpd for functions that call execFormatCmd or \"system\" with a sprintf'd command. \n\nLet me look for getNvram/setNvram + exec. Let me search httpd for \"execFormatCmd\" or function names containing \"exec\" or \"cmd\".\n\nActually, execFormatCmd appeared in httpd main. Let me trace who calls execFormatCmd with user data. execFormatCmd(format, ...) likely is a system() wrapper with va_args formatting. If any RPM handler calls execFormatCmd with attacker-controlled nvram values from a form, that's command injection.\n\nLet me search httpd for execFormatCmd callers.\n\nGiven one-trace-at-a-time is no longer an issue (last trace done), let me search.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me look at the specific promising finding: the minidlnad chunked-encoding memmove overflow (sub_40D1FC) as a memory-corruption candidate, and try to fuzz it dynamically.\n\nLet me think about what's most tractable to actually confirm with a PoC. \n\nGiven the environment, let me try fw_request_fuzz on minidlnad whole-binary. If minidlna's main needs IP detection, whole-binary fuzz will fail at startup. \n\nLet me instead reconsider: is there ANY binary that runs under qemu and parses attacker input? The ones that parse stdin/file would run. E.g., a media parser in minidlnad (libav) or the ffmpeg. But minidlnad is the host.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider dnsproxy more. It failed with recv 0. Why? dnsproxy's sub_401B38 does select on dns_sock which is set up in init. If init reads /tmp config and fails, dns_sock might be -1 → select fails → loop. Actually it returned immediately? The trace sent 29 bytes, recv 0. Maybe dnsproxy binds to 127.0.0.1 or a specific IP that the harness's loopback port mapping doesn't match.\n\nI think I'm at the point where I should record the strongest static finding. Let me be decisive and examine the httpd command injection.\n\nLet me find the httpd h","stream":true,"block":0}

