is fundamentally a forwarder: it reads config (resolv.conf upstream), sets up sockets, then loops. Under qemu without /etc/resolv.conf upstream it might still bind. But select didn't fire.\n\nOK. I'll make a decision: Given I cannot get dynamic verification on the main firmware daemons due to environment, and given the user wants confirmed findings, I'll do a focused static analysis on the most defensible pre-auth vulnerability and record it as static-only with strong evidence, being honest about reachability.\n\nActually, before recording static-only, let me reconsider if there's truly no dynamic path. Let me reconsider the httpd more. It failed because pthread_create failed. But the trace harness reported tcp_connect success on port 10080. That's contradictory unless the harness's environment provides different runtime. Actually the difference: fw_qemu_exec ran httpd directly and it failed with pthread_create. The fw_request_trace runs it in a coverage harness that may stub differently, resulting in bind success but request still unhandled.\n\nGiven repeated recv 0 for httpd, it's dead.\n\nLet me reconsider one more dynamic candidate: athadhoc (IN-001, UDP). It's a wireless adhoc tool that might bind UDP and parse. Small binary. Let me check if it runs. But ad-hoc tools usually are config-driven clients.\n\nActually, let me reconsider bpalogin — a PPPoE auth client. It does UDP. Client though.\n\nI think the pragmatic path: record the strongest static finding. But I should pick ONE that I'm confident is real. \n\nLet me reconsider the httpd command injection via a specific known-good pattern. Actually, let me reconsider — the task explicitly listed categories. A classic, well-studied TP-Link Archer C7 httpd command injection is in the \"Wizard\"/\"Setup\" cgi or the DDNS. Given I can't easily navigate the 4863-function httpd statically within budget, let me instead look at uclited, which is smaller (d61e67ed, has execFormatCmd with 44 callers) and is the config daemon that httpd talks to.\n\nHmm, but uclited is a local IPC (unix socket / TCP localhost). \n\nLet me reconsider the whole thing. Given the very substantial environment limitations, maybe the intended path is actually one of the high-scored memunsafe paths that CAN be driven — e.g., via fuzz. Let me reconsider ff_rtsp_connect / udp_open in libavformat. These are in a library, used by a binary. Which binary uses libavformat for RTSP? Possibly minidlnad (SSDP) or a separate \"media\" app. \n\nI'm spending too long deliberating. Let me make concrete progress on ONE candidate: the minidlnad chunked memmove or path traversal, and try to fuzz minidlnad's parser.\n\nActually, let me reconsider using fw_request_fuzz on minidlnad with function=0x40D1FC (the upnphttp request parser). The fuzz tool on MIPS may use whole-binary mode (preferred). But minidlnad needs IP detection to reach the parser.\n\nI think I should accept the environment limits and produce the best static findings. Let me analyze the strongest static candidate concretely and record it.\n\nLet me reconsider the httpd command injection. Let me look at execFormatCmd in httpd and find callers that pass network data. Let me look at the actual execFormatCmd function to confirm it's system().\n\nLet me get execFormatCmd source (httpd 0x5308cc) and the uclited one.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The user wants ME to find vulnerabilities. Given the environment is hard, the highest-value concrete result that I can defend: Let me analyze the httpd command injection more carefully by looking at execFormatCmd and a caller that uses request-derived data.\n\nLet me get httpd execFormatCmd source and call_trace inbound for it (qualified).\n\nActually time to be decisive. Let me examine execFormatCmd (httpd) to confirm it's a system() wrapper, then find a caller with form data. Given 88 callers, let me look at callers that are RPM handlers.\n\nLet me just record the strongest finding I can defensibly make. But I need call_chain + poc.\n\nLet me reconsider the minidlnad chunked overflow as the finding. In sub_40D1FC, the chunked transfer decoding: \n\n```\nv3 = a1+32 + a1+44 (start of request body)\ndest = v3\nloop:\n  a1+112 = strtol(nptr, &src, 16)  // chunk length in hex\n  if (!len or src==nptr) break\n  advance src to \\r\\n\n  src += 2\n  memmove(dest, src, len)","stream":true,"block":0}

