chain + poc.\n\nLet me reconsider the minidlnad chunked overflow as the finding. In sub_40D1FC, the chunked transfer decoding: \n\n```\nv3 = a1+32 + a1+44 (start of request body)\ndest = v3\nloop:\n  a1+112 = strtol(nptr, &src, 16)  // chunk length in hex\n  if (!len or src==nptr) break\n  advance src to \\r\\n\n  src += 2\n  memmove(dest, src, len)\n  dest += len\n```\nThis memmove copies `len` bytes (attacker-controlled hex chunk length) into dest, but dest is within the request buffer with no bounds check against buffer size. If the attacker declares a chunk length larger than the remaining buffer, this overflows. Also the next iteration reads `nptr = src + a1+112` (src + chunklen) — src was set to original src after memmove? Actually nptr = (char*)src + a1+112 where src is the pointer after the memmove... reads beyond.\n\nThis is a real heap overflow candidate in minidlna upnphttp chunked parsing (CVE? This is classic minidlna upnphttp.c). But minidlnad doesn't run under qemu. So I can only record static.\n\nGiven I cannot verify dynamically, and given the strict discipline about not overstating reachability, I'll record this as static-only with appropriate confidence (≤0.6 since memory corruption without checksec, and no trace).\n\nHmm, but the finding should be solid. Let me reconsider whether it's truly attacker-controlled and overflowing.\n\nActually, before recording, let me reconsider if maybe I should try harder to get a dynamic result. The minidlnad fatal is \"No IP address automatically detected\". What if I pass argv that sets friendly_name + forces an IP? minidlnad reads IP from interfaces via ioctl. In qemu-user there's no network device. Can't.\n\nLet me reconsider dnsproxy: it uses select on dns_sock. If dns_sock init failed (returns -1), select with rfds bit set at -1>>5 would be UB but likely just doesn't fire. Empty diff confirms no processing.\n\nI'm confident the environme","stream":true,"block":0}

