data: {"seq":496,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:50:56.154Z","text":"execFormatCmd: vsprintf(s, format, arg) with s[260] — format from callers. 88 callers in httpd. Command injection if a caller passes attacker data into format.\n\nTo find a concrete injectable caller, I need to identify which callers pass HTTP form data. This is a big manual effort. Given the difficulty and that httpd can't run dynamically (so I can't verify), recording a specific httpd cmd injection is risky without pinpointing an actual caller with attacker-controlled data.\n\nLet me step back and reconsider. I've done substantial recon. Given environment constraints, let me reconsider whether to record the minidlnad chunked overflow (a concrete, code-level real overflow) as the primary finding, since it's a well-characterized memory corruption with a clear PoC shape.\n\nBut minidlnad requires auth? No — UPnP is pre-auth. The chunked parsing is in the HTTP request parser reached from Process_upnphttp for any HTTP request to minidlnad's port (e.g., 8200). So pre-auth network reachable, static.\n\nHowever, confidence: memory corruption without layout confirm, no trace. Cap 0.6 even for observed; static-only ≤0.7 but memory-corruption without checksec ≤0.6. Let me record at 0.6 static-only.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the memmove overflow more carefully to make sure it's exploitable/bounded. dest is within the request buffer at [a1+32] (the full request). a1+44 is an offset. The loop copies chunk bodies to dest. There's no check that dest stays within buffer. Attacker controls chunk length. However, if the request buffer itself is the source and destination overlap, memmove is used (handles overlap). The overflow: attacker sends a chunked request where a chunk length declares more data than actually present → memmove reads and writes beyond. This is the classic minidlna CVE-2020-28926 / CVE-2020-12695 type (Heap overflow via chunk size).\n\nActually, I recall specific: minidlna upnphttp chunk handling 