data: {"seq":646,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:53:01.660Z","text":"uclited port 90 empty diff. So uclited's MiniServer isn't on 90, or uclited's main didn't even start the mini server under qemu (needs SSDP sockets / real iface). \n\nI've now spent a huge amount. Given all dynamic attempts failed (environment can't run the daemons interactively), I need to settle for strong static findings.\n\nLet me reconsider and be decisive. I'll record findings based on solid static analysis. But I should make them defensible.\n\nGiven the user's goals, let me pick the strongest static candidates I've concretely verified in code:\n\n1. minidlnad sub_40D1FC chunked-transfer overflow (CWE-787/122, heap overflow). Static, pre-auth UPnP. Confidence ~0.5-0.6.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I can find a cleaner command injection with concrete PoC in a specific handler. Given I haven't pinpointed one, recording a command injection without a specific caller is weak.\n\nLet me reconsider the minidlnad chunked overflow once more and record it. The code:\n```\nif ( a1+116 & 0x100 )  // chunked transfer encoding bit\n{\n  if (a1+112) { stuff }\n  v3 = a1+32 + a1+44;   // dest = buffer + offset\n  dest = v3;\n  for (nptr=v3; ; nptr = src + a1+112) {\n    a1+112 = strtol(nptr, &src, 16);\n    if (!a1+112 || src==nptr) break;\n    while (*src!=13 || *(src+1)!=10) src++;\n    src += 2;\n    memmove(dest, src, a1+112);\n    dest += a1+112;\n  }\n}\n```\nAttacker-controlled chunk length (a1+112, hex from body) copied via memmove to dest with no bound check on dest capacity → heap buffer overflow. Yes, this is CWE-122. Reachable via chunked HTTP POST to the UPnP HTTP endpoint pre-auth.\n\nBut the source/dest overlap: dest starts at body, src advances. Actually dest and src start at same point (v3). memmove(dest, src, len) where src was advanced past \\r\\n. Overlap possible but memmove handles it. The overflow: dest accumulates beyond the request buffer → writes past end.\n\nThis is defensible. But minidlnad's buffer — the request is read into a malloc'd upnphttp struct; a1+32 is the request buffer pointer. overflow writes into adjacent heap memory.\n\nHmm, but actually we need to know the actual request buffer bounds. The reading happens elsewhere (before sub_40D1FC — filling a1+32 buffer). Given chunked length is unbounded, overflow is real.\n\nLet me record this as a finding. But honestly without dynamic verification and precise layout, confidence low-moderate.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to instead record something I'm more sure about. \n\nLet me reconsider the httpd command injection angle more concretely by finding ONE caller. Given time, let me look at a very common injectable spot: the \"PingTest\"/\"Traceroute\" or \"WAN static route\" or \"DDNS\". \n\nActually, given all my dynamic attempts failed and I've spent enormous effort, let me reconsider whether the expectation is that I find SOMETHING recordable. I should record at least one defensible finding. The minidlnad chunked overflow is the most concrete code-level one I've read.\n\nBut wait — reachability. The finding rule: static-only requires no trace_id and confidence ≤0.7. Memory corruption without checksec ≤0.6. So I'll record static-only, confidence 0.55, severity high.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I should present my findings to the user in Chinese summarizing what I found and the environment limits, and record the minidlnad overflow. Given the extensive recon, that's the honest deliverable.\n\nBut let me reconsider whether to look a bit more at a command injection in a tractable target. Actually — let me reconsider vsftpd (FTP, port 21, IN-012). vsftpd is standard code, but the firmware may have a vendored vuln. vsftpd runs standalone and parses FTP commands (USER/PASS). It might run under qemu (vsftpd daemonizes but can run with -a). Actually vsftpd needs /etc/passwd and config.\n\nGiven the environment kills interactive daemons, let me accept static findings.\n\nLet me record the minidlnad chunked overflow finding now. Let me craft the finding with call_chain and PoC.\n\ncall_chain: 入口 Process_upnphttp (accept) → sub_40D1FC (HTTP 请求解析, 0x40d1fc) → memmove 无界拷贝 (a1+112 chunk length)\n\nActually I need the exact call chain from network entry to sink. Entry: Process_upnphttp → sub_40D1FC. Let me verify Process_upnphttp calls sub_40D1FC. The surface says Process_upnphttp is the accept handler and sub_40D1FC is a parser. That's a reasonable chain.\n\nPoC: Craft an HTTP request to minidlnad UPnP port with `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` and a chunk-size line declaring a huge length:\n```\nPOST / HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: x\\r\\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\\r\\n\\r\\nFFFFFFFF\\r\\nAAAA...\\r\\n0\\r\\n\\r\\n\n```\nThe strtol(\"FFFFFFFF\",..,16)=0xFFFFFFFF, memmove copies 4GB → overflow.\n\nActually strtol returns 0xFFFFFFFF (long). That's used as memmove length. Good PoC.\n\nLet me record this finding. I'll be honest that it's static_only, no trace (minidlnad can't run in qemu due to missing net iface).\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe record a second finding for the httpd execFormatCmd command injection is too speculative without a concrete caller.\n\nLet me record the minidlnad finding, then decide whether to continue hunting.\n\nActually, before recording, I want to strengthen the reachability claim for minidlnad pre-auth. minidlnad UPnP is pre-auth (SSDP + HTTP on 8200). It's a media server. The chunked parser is reached for POST/PUT requests with Transfer-Encoding. This is a genuine pre-auth memory corruption. Good.\n\nLet me also double check the bit 0x100 is set by chunked encoding header. That comes from header parsing (previous step in Process_upnphttp / header scan). I'll note it.\n\nLet me record the finding.\n\nWait — I should also verify minidlnad binary_md5 = 3e3258c907b4b461c90472e1fcd6cddb and path bin/minidlnad. Function addr 0x40d1fc, name sub_40D1FC.\n\nLet me record.","stream":false,"block":0}

