data: {"seq":694,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:53:45.330Z","text":"NoipDdnsRpmHtm reads form values (username, pwd, cliUrl, EnDdns) via httpGetEnv and stores into struct s, then calls swSetNoipDdnsCfg → ucSetNoipDdnsCfg. The values are strncpy'd into struct fields (s[1] username 64 bytes, s[17] pwd 64 bytes, s[33] cliUrl 128 bytes) — bounded by strncpy lengths.\n\nThe command execution happens later when DDNS starts (swDdnsStart → ucDdnsStart → which in uclited builds the update URL and runs wget/curl with these values). The injection would be in uclited's DDNS start code where username/password/hostname are put into a shell command (e.g., \"wget http://dynup","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":695,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:53:46.220Z","text":"NoipDdnsRpmHtm reads form values (username, pwd, cliUrl, EnDdns) via httpGetEnv and stores into struct s, then calls swSetNoipDdnsCfg → ucSetNoipDdnsCfg. The values are strncpy'd into struct fields (s[1] username 64 bytes, s[17] pwd 64 bytes, s[33] cliUrl 128 bytes) — bounded by strncpy lengths.\n\nThe command execution happens later when DDNS starts (swDdnsStart → ucDdnsStart → which in uclited builds the update URL and runs wget/curl with these values). The injection would be in uclited's DDNS start code where username/password/hostname are put into a shell command (e.g., \"wget http://dynupdate.no-ip.com/nic/update?hostname=...&myip=... -U user:pass\"). The classic TP-Link No-IP / DDNS command injection.\n\nSo the injection sink would be in uclited (ucDdnsStart/NoipDdns) building a system() command. Let me find that. The config is shared via IPC (ucSetNoipDdnsCfg sends to uclited). uclited receives and stores nvram, and when starting DDNS builds a command.\n\nThis requires authentication (DDNS is behind /userRpm/ which needs admin login). So it's authenticated command injection","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":696,"ts":"2026-08-28T08:53:46.598Z","text":"NoipDdnsRpmHtm reads form values (username, pwd, cliUrl, EnDdns) via httpGetEnv and stores into struct s, then calls swSetNoipDdnsCfg → ucSetNoipDdnsCfg. The values are strncpy'd into struct fields (s[1] username 64 bytes, s[17] pwd 64 bytes, s[33] cliUrl 128 bytes) — bounded by strncpy lengths.\n\nThe command execution happens later when DDNS starts (swDdnsStart → ucDdnsStart → which in uclited builds the update URL and runs wget/curl with these values). The injection would be in uclited's DDNS start code where username/password/hostname are put into a shell command (e.g., \"wget http://dynupdate.no-ip.com/nic/update?hostname=...&myip=... -U user:pass\"). The classic TP-Link No-IP / DDNS command injection.\n\nSo the injection sink would be in uclited (ucDdnsStart/NoipDdns) building a system() command. Let me find that. The config is shared via IPC (ucSetNoipDdnsCfg sends to uclited). uclited receives and stores nvram, and when starting DDNS builds a command.\n\nThis requires authentication (DDNS is behind /userRpm/ which needs admin login). So it's authenticated command injection. Still valuable (post-auth RCE), but the user also wants auth bypass.\n\nLet me find the actual sink in uclited where the DDNS command is built with execFormatCmd. Let me search uclited for the DDNS update command builder. \n\nGiven httpd can't be dynamically verified and this crosses into uclited IPC, this is getting very deep. Let me check the uclited side: search uclited for functions that build a \"wget\"/\"ddns\"/\"update\" command via execFormatCmd.\n\nLet me search uclited for \"execFormatCmd\" callers related to ddns, or a function containing \"wget\".","stream":true,"block":0}

